WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.169
In this video, we are going to go over the interface.

2
00:00:05.169 --> 00:00:09.169
So we will be looking at what all the different buttons do, and

3
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:13.169
how you can go about creating your first script.

4
00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:17.170
So we have opened up our blueprint

5
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.170
here, for example, and you should be seeing

6
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.170
a screen which looks something like this.

7
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.170
We'll go over each section here. To start with, let's have a look

8
00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:33.170
at the toolbar across the top here.

9
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.170
This is going to give us some kind of idea of the functionality.

10
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.390
So the first two buttons, which are pretty standard

11
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.390
amongst all of them, is the save.

12
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.390
It does what it says on the can. It just saves the file that we're working on

13
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:53.460
at that point in time. The one next to it might be

14
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.460
slightly less obvious, but this is going to allow us to browse to

15
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.740
the associated asset within the content browser.

16
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.739
So now, in order to get an asset into

17
00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:09.739
a level, you'll often need to just drag it from the content

18
00:01:09.739 --> 00:01:13.810
browser like this, but you might not be

19
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.810
in the correct directory, and projects can get quite complicated sometimes.

20
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.810
So an easy way to navigate to it

21
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.810
is just to click on this button here, and that's going to take us directly

22
00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.810
to the Blueprint and allow us to drag it into the level. You will often also

23
00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.810
see them in the details panel, where you're

24
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.810
going to be able to quickly browse just from

25
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.810
the reference in the details. Next up, you have the compile button.

26
00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.840
Now, what compiling usually

27
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.840
does when you're working with a program is convert

28
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.349
a piece of code into a runnable form.

29
00:01:54.349 --> 00:01:58.349
Now, Blueprints are slightly different. Mostly, this is

30
00:01:58.349 --> 00:02:02.349
going to be doing a lot of checking to see

31
00:02:02.349 --> 00:02:06.349
that what we have is runnable

32
00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:10.349
and then it's going to convert it into something that we can run and

33
00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:14.349
work within the level. You can see that at the moment we have a

34
00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:18.349
green tick next to our compile, and the word's good to go.

35
00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:22.349
So this means that this is working. This is fine.

36
00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:26.349
However, if I was to come into the coding section here,

37
00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:30.349
if I was to just add a section of code, I'm going to come off and do

38
00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:34.669
a create widget.

39
00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:38.669
Now we can see in the top left hand corner that we have

40
00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:42.669
a yellow compile button. And if you look at the

41
00:02:42.669 --> 00:02:46.669
tool tip, it says it's dirty and needs to be recompiled.

42
00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:50.669
So at this point, we need to push the button to compile.

43
00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:55.819
The third option is the red dot

44
00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:59.819
compile, and this means that this has failed and there is an error in it.

45
00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:03.819
If you look down, you've got compiler results, you'll see

46
00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:07.819
that it'll give you a specific message telling you what the problem is,

47
00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.819
and it'll also give you a button that means that you can navigate quickly.

48
00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:15.819
So say I'm far away, you can navigate quickly to the problem.

49
00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:19.819
In this instance, I have not actually selected a class

50
00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:23.819
on this widget that I want to create. Don't worry too much about that.

51
00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:28.719
We'll go over that in a minute. Again, we've got the three settings

52
00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:33.780
and green is the good one that means you're good to go. Next up, we have

53
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.780
difference hid. Now this is part of the source

54
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.780
control. We're not going to touch on this in this thing, but this is going to allow you to look

55
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.780
at previously saved versions of the blueprints.

56
00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.780
Next we have find. Pretty self-explanatory.

57
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.780
It's going to bring up a find results button here, and if I

58
00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.780
type in a word, it will search through the code for references

59
00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.780
to it. Now if I double-click, it's going to take me to

60
00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.930
the appropriate mode.

61
00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.930
Next up we have hide unrelated. Now I'll demonstrate this quickly,

62
00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.599
but what this does is say

63
00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:19.600
I had a program that had multiple notes,

64
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.860
something like this. If I have

65
00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.860
hide unrelated ticked, you can see with the node that I have selected

66
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.860
at any one time, any node which is not connected

67
00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.860
or which would not have an impact on the selected

68
00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.720
node is going to be slightly greyed out. I'll just delete this.

69
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.579
Next up we have the class settings.

70
00:04:46.579 --> 00:04:50.579
This goes back to us talking about inheritance. The class settings

71
00:04:50.579 --> 00:04:54.579
in the class defaults. These are going to be in where you

72
00:04:54.579 --> 00:04:58.579
can change or reset the variables

73
00:04:58.579 --> 00:05:02.579
or the settings from the parent class which you've inherited.

74
00:05:02.579 --> 00:05:06.579
Okay, simulation. This is actually hard

75
00:05:06.579 --> 00:05:10.579
to demonstrate without. I would just add a couple of things to the world

76
00:05:10.579 --> 00:05:16.209
so that we can see something. So what I've done

77
00:05:16.209 --> 00:05:20.209
is I've added a static mesh component for a cube, and

78
00:05:20.209 --> 00:05:24.209
I will add a rotating movement.

79
00:05:24.209 --> 00:05:28.209
These are just two components, actor

80
00:05:28.209 --> 00:05:32.209
components, which I've added in quickly to give us some functionality.

81
00:05:32.209 --> 00:05:36.209
If I was to now hit the simulate button, you can see

82
00:05:36.209 --> 00:05:40.209
within the viewport that the rotating movement

83
00:05:40.209 --> 00:05:44.300
is just rotating the actor within the world.

84
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:48.300
So I will just stop that there. That allows you to just

85
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.300
simulate so you can see what's going on easily

86
00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:57.040
within the viewport of the blueprints. Next

87
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.040
up are the play control buttons, and these are just exactly

88
00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.389
the same as you will find in the main screen of the editor.

89
00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:09.389
Now next up we have this drop

90
00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:13.389
down box here. This has no debug object selected.

91
00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:17.550
And this is going to allow us to

92
00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.550
view the code so that we can see how the code is working at

93
00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.550
any one time. The only problem is, in order to use this, we need

94
00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:29.550
to know which instance we are viewing. So at the moment

95
00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.550
we only have a singular instance within the world here.

96
00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:37.550
So if I do the drop down, you can see that I have the selected

97
00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.620
example. And so I'm going to select that one.

98
00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.620
Now I'm going to add a little bit of code onto

99
00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.970
Begin Play so that we can see it work. I'm just adding in three

100
00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.970
delays, and I'm going to make each of these one second long.

101
00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.970
And now, if I was to hit the play button, well with

102
00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:03.279
the debug example selected, you can see

103
00:07:03.279 --> 00:07:07.279
that it is giving us a visual interpretation of what the code is doing.

104
00:07:07.279 --> 00:07:11.310
I'm going to stop that and replay. See again,

105
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:15.310
we are seeing the line light up. This means you can actually

106
00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:19.310
watch the blueprint to see what's actually

107
00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:23.819
happening with it at any point in time. Next up, in the

108
00:07:23.819 --> 00:07:27.819
top left hand corner here, we have the components panel.

109
00:07:27.819 --> 00:07:32.339
Now this is going to be a list of all of the different

110
00:07:32.339 --> 00:07:36.339
components which have been added to your

111
00:07:36.339 --> 00:07:40.339
blueprint. So you can see here that we added in the

112
00:07:40.339 --> 00:07:44.370
cube, which is a static mesh component

113
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.370
which can be viewed now in the viewport here.

114
00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:53.740
We also added in the rotating movement. Now this is

115
00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:57.899
a good example of a

116
00:07:57.899 --> 00:08:01.899
component which has no visual aspect to it. There's not a

117
00:08:01.899 --> 00:08:05.899
child of an actor class. It doesn't have a transform.

118
00:08:05.899 --> 00:08:10.160
However, the cube itself does. It just

119
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:14.160
has some functionality which would be applied to the blueprint.

120
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.449
In the top left hand corner of the components field, there is a

121
00:08:18.449 --> 00:08:22.449
Add button. If you hit this, it's going to give you a list of

122
00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:26.449
all of the different components that you are going to be able to

123
00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:30.509
add. Below this is the My Blueprints

124
00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:34.990
section. Now, if you've done any coding before,

125
00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:38.990
you'd probably recognise this to look a bit like a header.

126
00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.990
In this, we are going to define what

127
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.990
different events and functions and macros

128
00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:50.990
and variables that are connected

129
00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:56.399
to the coding. This will be more useful, more

130
00:08:56.399 --> 00:09:00.399
valuable when we actually jump into the coding section of this.

131
00:09:00.399 --> 00:09:05.779
Now, on the right hand side, we have a details panel. The details

132
00:09:05.779 --> 00:09:09.779
panel is common across all of the different sections of

133
00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:13.779
Unreal Engine. This is a contextual menu.

134
00:09:13.779 --> 00:09:17.779
When you have different things selected, the details are going to

135
00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:21.779
change accordingly for the different settings that you've had. You can

136
00:09:21.779 --> 00:09:25.779
view this, for example, if I was to click on one of the components

137
00:09:25.779 --> 00:09:29.779
or one of the variables, we are going to get different values

138
00:09:29.779 --> 00:09:33.779
in the details panel, allowing us to change how they are set up.

139
00:09:33.779 --> 00:09:39.220
Now, in the centre, we have a

140
00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:43.250
viewport. Now, this viewport functions very

141
00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:47.250
much like the viewport that we all have inside

142
00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:52.370
the main Unreal Engine window.

143
00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:56.370
You are going to be able to move around it in a very similar way.

144
00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.370
So if I hold down the right hand mouse button, I'm going to be able to change the rotation

145
00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:04.399
of my editor camera, and whilst I have

146
00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:08.399
the right hand mouse button held down,

147
00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:12.399
I'll be able to use the W-A-S-D to move around

148
00:10:12.399 --> 00:10:17.649
the world to see things from a different angle. You'll find

149
00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:21.649
the majority of the other keys work in this as well. You'll also

150
00:10:21.649 --> 00:10:25.649
see that we have a number of different menus in the top left hand corner, in the top

151
00:10:25.649 --> 00:10:29.649
right hand corner. These will correspond to

152
00:10:29.649 --> 00:10:33.649
the similar menus in the main viewport.

153
00:10:33.649 --> 00:10:38.129
In the top left hand corner, we will have some settings so that we can

154
00:10:38.129 --> 00:10:42.129
change the way that this scene is displayed, and in

155
00:10:42.129 --> 00:10:46.129
the top right hand corner, we're going to be able to change the way we can

156
00:10:46.129 --> 00:10:50.129
interact with the scene. So the first thing that we've got are the different

157
00:10:50.129 --> 00:10:54.129
modes of changing between selecting objects,

158
00:10:54.129 --> 00:10:58.129
translating and moving objects, rotating and scaling.

159
00:10:58.129 --> 00:11:03.759
We are also going to have the

160
00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:07.759
snapping percentages on the right hand side. So by clicking on these

161
00:11:07.759 --> 00:11:12.019
buttons, you can turn them on or off to

162
00:11:12.019 --> 00:11:16.500
prevent the need to have to snap to those numbers. We also have the

163
00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.500
camera speed, which can be also controlled by

164
00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.500
right hand mouse button, and using the middle mouse button to roll

165
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:28.500
up and down to speed up how fast and slow the camera

166
00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.529
moves. Now next to the viewport, we will have

167
00:11:32.529 --> 00:11:36.850
two separate coding graphs.

168
00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:41.169
We will have the construction script and the event graph. Now

169
00:11:41.169 --> 00:11:45.389
if we are to click on these,

170
00:11:45.389 --> 00:11:49.389
we will see that we have a number of different

171
00:11:49.389 --> 00:11:53.389
nodes. So this is where we're going to be actually doing our

172
00:11:53.389 --> 00:11:59.019
coding. Now, navigating around

173
00:11:59.019 --> 00:12:03.019
this is an interesting topic. So

174
00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:07.149
the first thing is, if you want to move within these,

175
00:12:07.149 --> 00:12:11.149
if you place the mouse over the background and hold down

176
00:12:11.149 --> 00:12:15.309
the right hand mouse button, you'll see that the mouse cursor

177
00:12:15.309 --> 00:12:19.309
converts to a small hand. This will allow you to

178
00:12:19.309 --> 00:12:24.019
drag around the scene. Whilst this hand

179
00:12:24.019 --> 00:12:28.019
is present, you can scroll the middle mouse wheel

180
00:12:28.019 --> 00:12:32.019
in and out, and this will allow you to zoom in and

181
00:12:32.019 --> 00:12:37.809
zoom out of the code that you're looking at.

182
00:12:37.809 --> 00:12:41.809
Next up, we have the construction script. This functions in a very

183
00:12:41.809 --> 00:12:45.809
similar way to the event graph. It just fires at a slightly different

184
00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:50.740
time. What we will do in the next video is we're going

185
00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.740
to go over how you can create code and

186
00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:58.740
place nodes within the editor, and we will be going into these

187
00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:02.740
sections in much more detail then.
